.TH MUX 1 "2026-07-30" "mux 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mux \- a tmux session manager for agent-heavy, many-session work
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mux
.RI [ verb "] [" --bare "] [" NAME "] [" LAYOUT ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mux
builds and drives tmux sessions from declarative
.B layouts
and puts a live status bar on top: each session's coding-agent state at a
glance (idle, working, blocked), a per-host colour chip, and \(em through an
optional
.B context
hook \(em a marker for whichever side of a work / personal boundary a pane
sits on.
.PP
A session is created from a layout of the same name (or an explicit one), its
panes opened at a shared root, one running an
.BR agent .
Sessions are picked, cycled, and jumped-to by how much attention each agent
needs, so "who is waiting on me" is always visible.
.PP
mux ships a tmux fragment (see
.BR "TMUX INTEGRATION" )
that wires the status bar, key bindings, and hooks; the
.B mux
command manages sessions, layouts, themes, and the palette.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B mux
With no verb, pick a session to attach (an fzf picker, else a numbered menu).
.TP
.B mux go " [" \fINAME\fR "] [" \fILAYOUT\fR ]
Create, attach, or switch to session
.I NAME
(default: derived from the current directory), building it from
.I LAYOUT
if it is not up. The agent
.B continues
its most recent conversation.
.TP
.B mux resume " [" \fINAME\fR "] [" \fILAYOUT\fR ]
As
.BR go ,
but the agent
.B resumes
\(em it prompts to choose a conversation. Only meaningful when the session is
being built.
.TP
.B mux \-\-bare " ..."
Build the session but run neither the agent's
.I go
nor
.I resume
command \(em a plain shell in the agent pane.
.TP
.B mux ls
List sessions in the current context, each tagged with its agent-state glyph.
.TP
.B mux new " [" \fINAME\fR "] [" \fICOLOR\fR "] [" \fIROOT\fR ]
Write a new layout.
.RB ' \- '
means the default colour / no root.
.TP
.B mux save " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Snapshot the current session's shape (windows, panes, root, theme) to
.IR NAME .layout.
.TP
.B mux edit " [" \fINAME\fR ]
Open a layout in
.BR $EDITOR .
.TP
.B mux rename " [" \fIOLD\fR "] " \fINEW\fR
Rename a session and its matching layout.
.TP
.B mux theme " [" \fINAME\fR " | next | prev]"
Show, set, or cycle the current session's colour theme. With
.B \-p
(or
.BR \-\-persist ),
also write the choice into the session's layout.
.TP
.B mux next-blocked
Jump to the session whose agent has needed you longest \(em the loudest chip on
the strip. Repeat to walk down the urgency order.
.TP
.B mux hide " " \fISESSION\fR ", " mux show " " \fISESSION\fR
Hide or unhide
.I SESSION
for this client only \(em it drops off this attachment's status strip and out of
its
.RB ( "/" )
cycling. Per client, so monitors can differ.
.TP
.B mux show-all
Clear this client's hidden sessions.
.TP
.B mux reload
Re-source
.I tmux.conf
on every running mux server (recompiling the palette), so a config or theme edit
takes effect without a restart.
.TP
.B mux kill " " \fINAME\fR ", " mux kill-all
Tear down the named session, or (with a prompt) every session on this socket.
.SH LAYOUTS
A layout is a small declarative file,
.IR "$MUX_DIR/<name>" .layout,
read and validated in full before any tmux call. Directives are either
.B session-wide
(last-wins, order-independent) or
.B shape
(build the windows and panes in order):
.TP
.B root \fIDIR\fR
Working directory for every pane (a leading
.B ~
expands).
.TP
.B theme \fINAME\fR
The colour theme (see
.BR THEMES ).
.TP
.B agent \fINAME\fR
Which agent profile a
.B pane agent
runs (see
.BR AGENTS ).
.TP
.B notify \fIalways\fR | \fIaway\fR
Whether the agent notification fires always, or only while you are away.
.TP
.B window \fINAME\fR
Start a new window.
.TP
.B pane \fICMD\fR
A pane running
.IR CMD .
.B pane agent
runs the session's agent;
.B pane \fINAME\fR
runs a specific agent profile.
.TP
.B bottom \fIN\fR | \fIMIN\-MAX\fR
A full-width bottom shell pane, with a fixed or bounded height in lines.
.TP
.B include \fIREF\fR
Splice in
.IR REF .layout.
A user layout in
.B $MUX_DIR
wins over a shipped one (a
.B shape
in
.BR $MUX_SHARE ),
so
.RB ' "include shapes/code" '
pulls the shared vim | agent | shell shape from the package.
.PP
Shapes are reusable fragments under
.IR $MUX_SHARE/shapes .
.SH THEMES
The palette is data:
.IR $MUX_SHARE/themes/<name> .theme,
each naming up to six styles (bar, current-window chip, active/inactive borders,
copy-mode selection, prompt; only bar/window/accent are required). It is
compiled into tmux
.B @theme-*
options by
.RB ' "mux themes" ',
run from the tmux fragment at server start and re-pushed on drift, so a theme
edit needs no manual step.
.I $MUX_SHARE/themes/defaults
names the global default. A user override in
.I $MUX_DIR/themes
wins.
.SH AGENTS
An agent profile,
.IR $MUX_SHARE/agents/<name> .agent,
is two lines: a
.B go
command and a
.B resume
command, each a shell command mux runs as the agent pane's process. Claude is
the default; add a CLI by dropping in a profile.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Provided by the tmux fragment (prefix table unless noted):
.TP
.BR ( ", " )
Cycle to the previous / next visible session (skipping hidden ones).
.TP
.B b
Jump to the longest-blocked session (\c
.BR next-blocked );
repeat to walk the urgency order.
.TP
.B B
Toggle back to the last session.
.TP
.BR r ", " R
Refresh a wedged pane layout; \fBR\fR rebuilds a stuck-render pane.
.TP
.B Space
The explorer (\c
.BR choose-tree ).
.TP
.B click " (status)"
Click a session chip in the strip to jump straight to it.
.SH THE CONTEXT SEAM
mux core knows no particular notion of "context" (a work / personal split, a
namespace, a git host). An optional executable hook,
.IR $MUX_DIR/context ,
supplies one; with none installed, mux runs a single default context. Verbs:
.TP
.B context env " [" \fIPID\fR ]
Emit
.IR PID 's
context as whitelisted
.RI < key >
.RI < value >
lines:
.B socket
(tmux
.B \-L
suffix for isolation),
.B side
(an opaque token driving layout filtering and the marker),
.B label
and
.B banner
(the reminder text and its style),
.B theme
(the context's default theme).
.TP
.B context side \fIROOT\fR
The side a layout rooted at
.I ROOT
belongs to, or
.B shared
(visible in every context).
.TP
.B context sockets
Every non-default context server the hook can address (for
.B reload
and palette sync).
.PP
Output is parsed against the key whitelist, never eval'd. mux only
.B reflects
a context; whatever enforces a boundary lives in the integrator.
.SH TMUX INTEGRATION
Source the shipped fragments from your
.IR tmux.conf :
.PP
.nf
.RS
source-file ~/.local/share/mux/mux.tmux
source-file ~/.local/share/mux/mux-opinions.tmux   # optional
.RE
.fi
.PP
.I mux.tmux
is required \(em the status bar, feature bindings, and resize hooks; it reaches
mux's helpers only as
.RB ' "mux <verb>" ',
so it carries no paths.
.I mux-opinions.tmux
is optional ergonomics (mouse, scroll routing, navigation).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MUX_DIR
User config / overrides (layouts, themes overrides, the
.I context
hook). Default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mux ,
else
.IR ~/.config/mux .
.TP
.B MUX_SHARE
Shipped package data (themes, shapes, agents, the tmux fragments). Default the
.I share
sibling of the
.B mux
binary's prefix.
.TP
.B MUX_CACHE
Per-socket palette stamp directory. Default
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/mux .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/<name>.layout
User session layouts (\c
.BR "mux new" ", " save " write here)."
.TP
.I $MUX_DIR/context
The optional context hook.
.TP
.I $MUX_SHARE/{themes,shapes,agents}
Shipped palette, shape fragments, and agent profiles.
.TP
.I $MUX_SHARE/mux.tmux
The tmux integration fragment.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open (or attach) a session for the current project, agent continuing:
.PP
.RS
.B mux go
.RE
.PP
Write a layout rooted at a repo, then bring it up resuming a conversation:
.PP
.nf
.RS
mux new api - ~/src/api
mux resume api
.RE
.fi
.PP
Jump to whoever has been blocked longest, then back:
.PP
.RS
prefix
.BR b " \(em ... \(em prefix " B
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR valet-key (1)
